    "content": "Approximately 500 displaced families who were relocated to a new camp about 20 kilometers east of Hargeisa, Somaliland's capital, are reporting serious health and living challenges. The families were moved to Qasali camp in June by Hargeisa municipal authorities, according to Radio Ergo. Many came from the Nasa-hablood camp near the city, while others are newly displaced after losing their livestock to drought.\n\nThe new site is situated near a waste disposal ground where household rubbish and waste from slaughtered animals are dumped. Residents say the proximity to the dump is causing health problems. In addition to environmental concerns, the families face severe food shortages and a lack of basic services such as clean water, sanitation, and healthcare.\n\nThe relocation was carried out by local authorities as part of efforts to manage informal settlements around Hargeisa. However, the new location has introduced new hardships for already vulnerable families. Many of those moved had previously fled conflict or drought, and the move has not resolved their underlying needs.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this situation highlights the ongoing challenges faced by displaced populations in the Horn of Africa. Diaspora communities often send remittances to support relatives in camps, and news of deteriorating conditions can increase pressure on families abroad. Additionally, it underscores the need for sustainable solutions that address both immediate humanitarian needs and long-term resettlement.\n\nThe lack of essential services and exposure to waste pose particular risks to children, the elderly, and those with pre-existing health conditions. Without intervention, the situation could worsen, leading to disease outbreaks and further displacement. Humanitarian agencies and local authorities have not yet publicly responded to the concerns raised by residents.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/10/dirty-conditions-and-lack-of-essential-services-trouble-idp-families-near-hargeisa/",
    "source": "radioergo.org",
