    "id": "news-1792050694735-7",
    "title": "Somali Parliament Speaker Attends 9th Anniversary of Soobe Bombing",
    "original_title": "Guddoomiyaha Golaha Shacabka oo Ka Qaybgalay Xuska 9 Sano Guuradii Ka soo Wareegtay Qaraxii Soobe",
    "slug": "somali-parliament-speaker-attends-9th-anniversary-of-soobe-bombing",
    "summary": "Speaker of the Somali Federal Parliament, Abdulkadir Mohamed Nur (Jama), participated in a memorial event marking nine years since the October 14, 2017 Soobe junction bombing in Mogadishu. The attack killed and injured many civilians, and officials described it as a terrorist act by al-Shabaab.",
    "content": "The Speaker of the People's Assembly of the Somali Federal Parliament, Abdulkadir Mohamed Nur (Jama), took part in a memorial gathering on Tuesday to mark the ninth anniversary of the October 14, 2017, bombing at the Soobe junction in Mogadishu. The event honored the Somalis who lost their lives and those who were wounded in the attack. Officials at the ceremony characterized the incident as a terrorist act carried out by al-Shabaab, which resulted in significant loss of life and property.\n\nThe Soobe junction bombing, one of the deadliest single attacks in Somalia's history, occurred on October 14, 2017, when a truck bomb exploded in a busy area of the capital. The blast killed hundreds of civilians and injured many more, devastating families and communities. In the years since, the site has been commemorated annually by government officials, civil society, and ordinary citizens seeking to remember the victims and reaffirm their rejection of violent extremism.\n\nIn his remarks, Speaker Abdulkadir Mohamed Nur stated that October 14 remains a day of sorrow and grief for the Somali people. He emphasized the importance of unity and resilience in the face of such tragedies. The memorial served as a somber reminder of the ongoing threat posed by al-Shabaab, which continues to carry out attacks despite sustained military operations against it.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the Soobe bombing struck a particularly deep chord. Many diaspora members lost relatives or friends in the attack, and the event galvanized fund-raising and advocacy efforts abroad. Annual commemorations like this one provide a focal point for diaspora communities to mourn collectively, raise awareness about the security situation in Somalia, and support reconstruction and humanitarian initiatives back home.\n\nThe ninth anniversary comes as Somalia continues to grapple with security challenges, including recent attacks in Mogadishu and other regions. The federal government has reiterated its commitment to defeating al-Shabaab and protecting civilians. However, the persistence of such violence underscores the need for continued international support and local resilience. For the diaspora, events like this highlight the importance of remaining engaged in Somalia's stabilization and development.\n\nAs the country marks nine years since the tragedy, the memory of the victims serves as a call to action for peace and reconciliation. The Somali diaspora, with its resources and global reach, plays a vital role in supporting these efforts. The commemoration in Mogadishu is a reminder that the pain of Soobe is shared across borders and generations, and that the pursuit of a stable and prosperous Somalia remains a collective responsibility.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/15/guddoomiyaha-golaha-shacabka-oo-ka-qaybgalay-xuska-9-sano-guuradii-ka-soo-wareegtay-qaraxii-soobe/",
    "source": "goobjoog.com",
