    "content": "A political dispute in Somalia's northern Sanaag region has deepened between President Abdikadir Ahmed Aw-Ali, known as Firdhiye, and Vice President Abdirahman Yusuf Jibril, known as Abwaanka. The disagreement centers on the filling of parliamentary seats reserved for the Sanaag community. The two leaders have publicly exchanged accusations, and the rift shows no sign of resolution.\n\nThe conflict reportedly stems from differing interpretations of how seats should be distributed among sub-clans in Sanaag. Tensions have been building for weeks, with local elders and politicians attempting to mediate. Neither the president's nor the vice president's office has issued a formal statement detailing their respective positions.\n\nThis is not the first time the two leaders have clashed. Since taking office, Firdhiye and Abwaanka have disagreed on several governance issues, including resource allocation and security appointments. The current standoff is viewed by observers as the most serious challenge to their working relationship.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, especially those with ties to Sanaag, the dispute is a source of concern. Many diaspora members fund development projects and send remittances to the region. A prolonged political impasse could hamper service delivery and discourage investment. Diaspora organizations have historically played a role in mediating local conflicts and may be called upon again.\n\nSanaag has been relatively stable compared to other parts of Somalia, but political infighting risks undermining that stability. The international community, including the United Nations, has emphasized the importance of peaceful resolution of such disputes. Local civil society groups are urging both leaders to prioritize the interests of the community.\n\nAs of now, no timetable has been set for further talks. The situation remains fluid, and stakeholders are watching closely. The outcome will likely affect the region's representation in the federal parliament and its overall political trajectory.",
