    "content": "Mogadishu, October 14, 2026 — Somalia's federal House of the People approved the Sexual and Ethical Crimes Bill during its 24th session of the 8th parliamentary term. The vote, held at the parliamentary headquarters in Mogadishu, saw 132 MPs in favor, 3 opposed, and 5 abstentions, with 140 legislators present. The session was chaired by Speaker Abdulqadir Mohamed Nur (Jamac), alongside Second Deputy Speaker Abdullahi Omar Abshir.\n\nThe bill's passage follows a legislative process that began earlier this year, driven by calls from civil society and international partners to strengthen legal protections against sexual violence. Somalia has faced longstanding challenges in prosecuting such crimes due to gaps in the penal code. The new legislation aims to define offenses and set penalties, though details of its provisions have not yet been fully disclosed.\n\nThis development is significant for the Somali diaspora, many of whom have advocated for improved human rights and legal accountability in their homeland. Diaspora organizations have frequently highlighted the need for robust legal frameworks to protect vulnerable populations, and the bill's approval is seen as a step toward addressing impunity.\n\nThe bill now requires approval from the Upper House and the President before becoming law. If enacted, it could mark a notable shift in Somalia's legal landscape, potentially influencing how such crimes are handled and prosecuted across the country.",
